  "query_preview": "..\nIssued at 300 AM EDT Mon Aug 10 2026\n\nCurrently-Tonight... KMLB radar imagery shows dry conditions across \neast central Florida. GOES-16 satellite imagery and local \nobservations indicate mostly clear skies. Temperatures are currently \nin the mid 70s to low 80s with dew points in the mid 70s to low 80s. \nWinds are generally light and variable. Analysis charts show a 594dm \n500 mb ridge over Florida and the Deep South. \n\nThe 500mb ridge is expected to reach 597dm over the state of Florida \ntoday which is at the maximum percentile for this time of year. \nIsolated to scattered showers (20-40% chance) and lightning storms\nare forecast to develop this afternoon and evening as collisions \noccur between sea breezes and outflow from previous \nshowers/lightning storms. Rain rain chances are expected to \ndiminish by around 8PM. Hi-res guidance shows MLCAPE up to \n1,500-4,000 J/kg, 0-6km up to 10-25kts, PWATs between 1.90-2.30\", \nand conditionally unstable mid-level lapse rates. Therefore, the \npotential exists for isolated strong storms capable of occasional \nto frequent lightning strikes, wind gusts up to 30-50mph, and \nheavy rainfall (up to 1-3\"). Temperatures will increase with \nafternoon highs in the low to mid 90s with heat index values up to\n102-107F degrees for most of east central Florida today. A Heat \nAdvisory has been issued for Okeechobee and Martin counties from \n11AM to 8PM for heat index values up to 108-110F degrees. Remember\nto take frequent breaks from the heat, drink plenty of fluids, \nwear light colored clothing, and shift outdoor work away from the \nhottest parts of the day between the hours of 11am to 4pm. Near to\nabove normal lows in low 70s to near 80F degrees are forecast \novernight.\n\nTuesday-Thursday... The mid/upper level ridge is expected to shift \nwest across the Deep South through mid to late week as a longwave \ntrough deepens over the western Atlantic. Drier conditions are \nexpected with drying in the mid levels as another plume of Saharan \nDry layer advects over the state of Florida mid to late week. One \nthing to watch will be air quality as this occurs with the CAMS \nindicating an Aerosol Optical Depth (AOD) value of 0.6-1.25 (Very\nHigh to Extreme haze) on Tuesday over the Orlando Metro. However,\nthose values appears high when compared to the GEOS model, but \nit's something to keep in mind. Isolated to widely scattered \nshowers (20- 30% chance) and lightning storms are forecast to \ndevelop Tuesday afternoon and evening as the east coast sea breeze\npushes inland and collides with a weak west coast sea breeze, as \nwell as outflow from previous showers/lightning storms. A similar \nforecast is expected Wednesday and Thursday with slightly higher \nrain chances (20-40% chance Wednesday and 20-30% chance Thursday) \nas moisture increases in the mid levels. Guidance shows \ninstability coupled with weak shear, conditionally to absolutely \nunstable mid level lapse rates, and PWATs between 1.80-2.20\" which\nis sufficient for isolated storms capable of occasional to \nfrequent lightning strikes, wind gusts up to 30-50mph, and heavy \nrainfall (up to 1-3\"+). Afternoon highs in the low to mid 90s with\nheat index values near heat advisory criteria (heat index values \nup to around 108F degrees) are forecast each day through at least \nFriday. \n\nFriday-Sunday... A longwave ridge is expected to build over the \neastern CONUS Friday and into the weekend. Deterministic guidance \nand Ensembles are in good agreement that 500mb heights will reach \n597dm (maximum percentile for this time of year) over the southern\nUS and the state of Florida. Thus, the heat is expected to \ncontinue with afternoon highs generally in the low to mid 90s each\nday and lows in the mid 70s to around 80 degrees. At least a \nModerate to Major HeatRisk is expected each day with portions of \nLake county in an Extreme HeatRisk. Isolated to scattered showers \n(20-40% chance) and lightning storms are forecast through Saturday\nwith mostly dry conditions on Sunday. West-southwest winds are \nforecast to back onshore each afternoon and evening with the east \ncoast sea breeze.",
